Vendored third-party software
=============================

@visimer/core
-------------
Version:  1.1.2 (pinned exact in package.json devDependencies)
License:  MIT
Source:   https://github.com/inkeep/visimer (packages/core)
Runtime dependencies: none.

@visimer/dom
------------
Version:  1.1.2 (pinned exact in package.json devDependencies)
License:  MIT
Source:   https://github.com/inkeep/visimer (packages/dom)
Runtime dependencies: @visimer/core only.

Both packages are ESM-only. They are bundled offline into the committed
static/vendor/visimer.bundle.js (a single IIFE exposing the global
`Visimer`) by the `vendor:visimer` npm script, so the app keeps its
no-runtime-build-step contract:

  node_modules/.bin/esbuild static/vendor/visimer.entry.js \
    --bundle --format=iife --global-name=Visimer --minify \
    --outfile=static/vendor/visimer.bundle.js

`static/vendor/visimer.entry.js` re-exports only the symbols the app
uses, so `window.Visimer` stays narrow:

  export { MermaidWysiwygEditor, parse } from "@visimer/core";
  export { MermaidCanvasView } from "@visimer/dom";

Provenance
----------

Reproducibility rests on the exact version pins in package.json (both
packages pinned `1.1.2`, no caret) plus the checksums recorded here.
package-lock.json is untracked (gitignored), so it is not part of the
repository and cannot serve as the recorded record; these hashes are.

  @visimer/core@1.1.2  integrity: sha512-do8MDqlLQeDRI1fy2PUTVnzBkLrzpaq260UX1WrtE/3XtSoLge4T5oY+cvSXFyINhYiYKB9tgetkx+o14LFrjA==
  @visimer/dom@1.1.2   integrity: sha512-Ir+Nl5vRgZA8rQwjGEQrC9ijOkdR0BmD3EjjqU/VedgK0lxleo5dKj8YIoHiocEDS1hlQwmzRZKIKg8MuV2FJw==

  static/vendor/visimer.bundle.js
    size:    147493 bytes
    sha256:  1096eb5ffdcd474b77e5aafc0759980cfdb004d010190e1b76f2802a6fa0337b

The tarball `integrity` values above are the sha512 SRI hashes npm records
for the pinned distributions. Re-running `npm run vendor:visimer` against
those exact versions must reproduce the bundle and its sha256.

Only the JS bundle is vendored. `@visimer/dom` ships no stylesheet; it
injects its own <style> element at runtime, so there is no visimer.css.

